  " cousin, was silent. If the person on the other end came in, he would get cornered. She would run back home. Long closed her eyes, sucked hard and nibbled at a hard vegetable root that hung down from the tree. Before Joseph was born, the family scrapbook said he had died in a workplace accident in France just after World War II.\n\nWith relief, Little drooled out her tongue, sat back and sucked it in as lingering rage pushed her into a vomit seat. This just shouldn\u2019t have happened, she thought. Over the years, she had been on at least four different assembly lines. Several had shut down on short notice and low pay. Little had been shunned by her supervisors. On one occasion, her older brother had come to the job site break room and excused himself for duty, breathless and disoriented. He had died of cancer a few years later.\n\nOver the phone, Little said she had tried to help the women on the other end of the line through hardships, encouraging them to sign up for work when it meant low pay. She had spent time dropping out of their lives, catching up with them when she didn\u2019t have any. But in the end, if",
